- THE COMPOSITE HAS ITS OWN CLOCK AND NOW SAYS SO (P0-instant-sync, fixed 2026-08-25). It is a second
  file, and its zero is not the record press: v1's starts when its MediaRecorder starts, after the
  <video> elements, canvas, audio graph and write stream exist (134-215 ms in); v2's starts at
  whichever of audio/video first reached the worker (66-90 ms in). CompositeRecording.startOffsetMs
  declares it — produced by BOTH engines, rebased with the channels — and both packet-copy paths place
  copied video at its true instant (compose/compositeTime.ts owns the conversion; ABSENT = 0 = the old
  assumption, kept for takes recorded before the field existed). Before, both assumed composite time
  IS recording time: instant measured 185 ms of A/V offset on v2 and 244.8 on v1; it now measures
  48.6-75.8 (v2) and 55.7-57.8 (v1), INSIDE the render's own spread on the same takes. Gated: the
  oracle bands the instant path at ≤90 ms symmetric and fails a run where the unedited export quietly
  took the render. Same fix retired the "smart cut sits one frame ahead" story — that was this clock,
  and o5cut now reports compositeOffsetFrames 0.
- THE EXPORT HAS THREE PATHS and EditorScreen picks in this order: INSTANT (unedited + composite +
  default tier → packet copy), SMART CUT (O5c — a trim/cut-only edit changes no pixel, so the
  composite's packets are copied and only [cut → next keyframe] is re-encoded; measured 5.5× faster
  than rendering, 684 ms on a 30 s take, 94 % of frames copied — DEFAULT ON since 2026-08-25 (O5-flip),
  `?smartcut=0` reverts; it declines a v1 composite outright because MediaRecorder's avcC is not ours
  to match, and the render takes those),
  and the FULL RENDER, which is also the fallback for both. The ladder itself is compose/choose.ts,
  driven by BOTH the editor and the oracle so the gate measures the path a user actually gets.
  The render RUNS IN A WORKER by default (O5a): same code in-thread as the fallback, and the
  certified audio path moved off Web Audio (AudioSample, not AudioBuffer) to get there — proven by the
  fidelity oracle, and by all four A/B lanes producing byte-identical files. 60 s edited take: 6.00×
  realtime in the worker vs 5.93× in-thread.
- THE EXPORT'S FLOOR IS DECODE, MEASURED: on a 12 s 1080p edited take the loop waits 1295 ms on decode,
  450 ms drawing and 28 ms on the encoder, against 962 ms to decode the same frames alone. The encoder
  was never the stall (mediabunny already queues it 4 deep). Pipelining it further was built, measured
  and deleted. `npm run exp -- o5` prints the split and the floor.
- exports STREAM TO DISK (O1): the muxer holds 4 MB regardless of length — a 30-min export peaks at
