  stalls, the frame table with page numbers, a pointer trail), then one frame per page as JPEG with a
  caption band that names the recording, the frame and its time. NO video track anywhere in it.
  THE TWO THINGS PO'S FIRST FILE GOT WRONG, both fixed and both measured on that same 97 s take:
  it never said what it was (an AI opened it and asked what to do with it), and it dropped the
  moments — 39 frames for 97 s, with typing, a button activating, a click and a tab switch all inside
  one 5.5 s gap. Now 165 frames, median gap 0.4 s, worst 2.9 s, whole take covered.
  WHAT IS DERIVED: 8 looks a second; content threshold 0.12 % of a 160×90 luma grid (~17 cells — a
  typed word) which is only safe because THE POINTER IS MASKED OUT of that metric rather than hidden
  under a high threshold; MOTION SUSPENDS THE PACE so an animation comes back as a sequence (needs 2
  consecutive moving samples — one change is an event, not an animation — and capped at 1.2 s so a
  scroll is throttled); the frame budget scales with the take's own length (2.5/s, 96-300) and is
  spread by a pace read off what is left of the take plus a burst credit that shrinks to zero by the
  end. WHAT THE READERS ACTUALLY ACCEPT, checked 2026-08-24 and not to be recalled again: Claude
  32 MB / 600 pages on 1M-context models (100 pages is the 200k-context figure), Gemini 50 MB /
  1000 pages at a flat 258 tokens/page. Pages are cheap; TOKENS are the binding cost (~800 per page),
  which is why the budget is a spend decision and there is a hard stop at 28 MB.
  Cursor immunity holds: a cursor wandering a still page for 12 s is ONE page out of 96 samples, a
  caret is none. Price: ~144k tokens for a 97 s UI take vs ~2.3M for the same take as video frames,
