- THE DEFAULT CAPTURE ENGINE IS v2 (O4, flipped 2026-08-24 — AWAITS PO REAL-TAKE VERIFICATION).
  v2 = worker + WebCodecs + WebGL2 + fragmented MP4 to its own SyncAccessHandle, frame-driven.
  v1 (MediaRecorder) remains the full ladder underneath: capability fallback (Apple WebKit and
  Firefox lack MediaStreamTrackProcessor -> v1 from the start), start-failure fallback (v2 throws ->
  v1 takes the take), and the mid-take watchdog (sustained <12 fps after first output -> composite
  refused, take renders from raw channels). `?engine=v1` / localStorage revert outright.
  WHY, all measured: v2 28.4 fps at 1080p vs v1 29.3 (gate >=28) - main-thread capture cost 0 vs
  198 ms of long tasks - whole-browser CPU peak 127 % vs 196 - oracle sync 33-48 ms vs ~60 - it
  drains an encoder it owns at stop. The three-session "2-10 fps wall" was the INSTRUMENT: a fresh
  Chrome process's first VideoEncoder pays a multi-second init (PER LAUNCH, not per profile), every
  rig take fit inside it, and the old cumulative-fps watchdog killed takes mid-init. Production
  pays the init at MOUNT now (prearm.ts -> encoderWarm.ts, proven to carry to the worker: 28.2 fps /
  13 ms / 0 drops fully cold); the watchdog clock starts at first output (compositorWatchdog.ts);
  o4step2 warms before measuring ({"cold":true} for the transient); recovery removes the orphaned
  composite blob at salvage. Flip gates all green: 287 tests - oracle PASS on the v2 default AND
  --engine=v1 - real-Chrome prod QA 8/8 with 0 console errors, export 504 ms (v1 historical
  505-508) - arming latency A/B-proven unaffected by the mount warm - armcancel green.
  O4-POLISH remains, not flip-blocking: preview from the compositor's own output (both engines
  still pay the preview <video> decodes today), sync <=20 ms anchor work, an e2e encoder-wedge
  case, and the 4K row in production shape (the rig feeds uncapped 4K, production caps at 1080p).
- THE TAIL IS KEPT, ON BOTH FILES A TAKE PRODUCES (2026-08-23). The composite used to lose 2734 ms
  off the end of a 4K take; it now loses 43-320 ms. stop() kills the painting first, then PROBES the encoder with requestData() until it
  answers empty (waiting for the byte flow to go quiet looks equivalent and is not — under load this
  recorder emits four chunks in ten seconds, so silence is its normal state and proves nothing). The
  drain recovers 1.0-1.1 MB per take that used to be discarded; a healthy 1080p take is unchanged
